      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "Local, not systemic, complications associated with intravenous regional sympathetic block (IVRS) are very rare. The authors report the complication that occurred in a 70-year-old man who sustained an injury of the left thumb."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The second IVRS using reserpine and lidocaine, 1 week after the first IVRS, was performed for pain relief."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "About 1 hour after the operation, an entirely localized blister developed in the dorsolateral aspect of the thumb. The palmar side appeared viable 2 weeks after the operation, which guaranteed intact survival of the thumb, but 2 months later the patient reported that he had the thumb amputated at an outside hospital."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "When IVRS is applied to the extremities with compromised vasculature, or with an altered neuroanatomy, unpredictable and counterproductive mishaps may occur."
        }
      ]},
